  "slug": "evolutionary-rescue-adaptation-0377",
  "summary": "New preprints link genomic architecture of rapid adaptation to population persistence, extinction and climate-response prediction across insects, fish and plants.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how the genetic architecture of adaptation determines persistence under abrupt or sustained environmental change. In cowpea seed beetles, rapid, repeatable rescue on lentil relies on large non-recombining haploblocks on chromosomes 1 and 9. In outdoor Drosophila under insecticide stress, half of populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation, with pre-adaptation implicated in preventing extinction. Related work addresses selection through time and across environments: seasonal adaptive tracking in Drosophila suzukii, pooled-sequencing of chronotype selection lines, and gradient-based GWAS in Boechera stricta suggesting projected hot, dry climates may exceed adaptive capacity. Stickleback studies separate within- and transgenerational plasticity architectures and show sex-specific responses to paternal predation cues. What is new is the joint treatment of haplotype structure, contingency, and plasticity in outcomes of rescue.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
